/** \page DFPTPhonons Primitive-cell DFPT phonons

The `jdftx` executable can calculate force matrices on a uniform phonon q mesh
without constructing an electronic supercell. The implementation follows the
Sternheimer response and ionic-potential derivatives of Baroni et al.,
Rev. Mod. Phys. 73, 515 (2001), Secs. II.C.1 and II.D, in particular Eqs. (85)-(88).
Both k+q and k-q responses are retained. The k mesh and phonon q mesh can be
chosen independently; shifted occupied states are solved automatically in the
unperturbed self-consistent potential.

Add the following to an insulating primitive-cell input:
\code
symmetries none
coulomb-interaction periodic
electronic-minimize nIterations 300 energyDiffThreshold 1e-12
perturb-minimize nIterations 300 residualTol 1e-7 residualDiffThreshold 0
phonon-dfpt 4 4 4
dump-name Si.$VAR
\endcode
Run `jdftx -i Si.in -o Si.out`. The q mesh is Gamma centered. By default, only
symmetry-inequivalent q points are solved; the complete grid is reconstructed
before Fourier transformation. q coordinates are fractional reciprocal-lattice coordinates;
ionic displacement directions are Cartesian, in bohr. Insulating calculations
include only occupied bands; smeared calculations also need empty bands.
Atomic masses come from the species masses, as in the existing
supercell phonon driver. Set a sufficiently converged electronic k mesh and
plane-wave cutoff, and converge the phonon q mesh separately.

For a 2D material with vacuum along the third lattice direction, use
`coulomb-interaction Slab 001` in both the SCF and DFPT inputs, and for example
`phonon-dfpt 6 6 1`. The truncated lattice vector must be perpendicular to the
periodic plane. Keep q-grid dimension 1 along that vector; all three Cartesian
ionic displacements, including out-of-plane modes, are computed. The slab
implementation uses the unregularized finite-q truncated Hartree kernel and
an analytic 2D Ewald ionic Hessian. To enable embedding, add
`coulomb-truncation-embed c0 c1 c2` in both inputs, with the center in the
coordinate system selected by `coords-type`. For graphene centered at z=0,
`coulomb-truncation-embed 0 0 0` is appropriate. Embedded Hartree response is
expanded to the doubled slab grid; ionic derivatives use Gaussian range
separation and act before embedding. The SCF and DFPT must also use the same
embedding center, ion margin and FFT grid. External electric fields and fluid
embedding are not supported.
Keep sufficient vacuum for the charge distribution and the Coulomb ion margin.
Electronic kpoint-folding must also be specified in the DFPT input and match
the SCF checkpoint; `phonon-dfpt-initial-state` does not inherit it.

The current scope is periodic or slab, nonmagnetic systems with scalar,
fully occupied bands or finite-width Fermi/Gauss smearing, an analytic LDA/GGA XC kernel, and nonrelativistic
norm-conserving pseudopotentials without nonlinear core corrections (for example,
SG15). Ultrasoft potentials, nonlinear core corrections, spinors, exact
exchange, DFT+U, meta-GGAs, fluids, vdW pair corrections and chargeballs are not
supported in this primitive-cell path. Incompatible inputs are rejected. The
existing `phonon` executable and its `useVPT` supercell mode remain available.
Do ionic or lattice optimization in a separate calculation first. Restart files
can be used, but the phonon driver still minimizes the ground state once.

For metals and gapless systems, add `elec-smearing Fermi 0.01` (width in Hartree)
or `elec-smearing Gauss 0.01` and include empty bands with `elec-n-bands`.
The highest stored band must have occupation below 1e-6 at every source and
offset k point; otherwise the calculation stops with a request for more bands.
Converge both the k mesh and smearing width for the physical system. An SCF
density solve is recommended to converge the partially occupied source bands.
The metallic response follows Sec. II.C.4, especially Eqs. (69)-(70) and (75):
occupation divided differences include their finite coincident-energy limit,
with energy-ordering smearing to partition each pair without double counting,
while Sternheimer equations account for states outside the stored subspace.
At Gamma a DOS projection includes the Fermi-level shift and enforces fixed
electron number. These are derivatives of the smeared electronic free energy
at fixed smearing width. Fixed chemical potential, Methfessel-Paxton and cold
smearing are currently rejected. The q/displacement symmetry reductions,
offset-state reuse and symmetry-reduced SCF restarts also work with smearing;
SCF and DFPT restart inputs must use identical smearing settings and band count.

Q reduction detects space-group operations independently of the electronic
symmetry setting, which must remain `symmetries none`. Only operations preserving
the entire q grid, electronic k mesh and weights, FFT sampling and ground-state
Hamiltonian are retained. Atom permutations include their integer cell shifts;
the resulting Bloch phases are essential for nonsymmorphic operations. All
equivalent mappings, including operations preserving a representative q, are
averaged. Time reversal is also used when the electronic k mesh is invariant
under k to -k. The log reports the number of representatives and response solves.
Atomic displacement responses are reduced by the little group of each q by default. Their symmetry images,
including complex cell phases and antiunitary time reversal, span the Cartesian
displacement space; the remaining force-matrix columns are reconstructed by
linear algebra. Set `phonon-dfpt-displacement-symmetries no` to solve all directions.
Set `phonon-dfpt-symmetries no` to calculate every q for a comparison.

Set `phonon-dfpt-k-symmetries yes` (default no) to also reduce the electronic response mesh
for each Cartesian displacement. Orbit weights enter the response equations;
the induced density is averaged with the displacement's symmetry character.
Full-mesh wavefunction responses are reconstructed with band sewing matrices
before force contractions, including arbitrary gauges in degenerate bands.
For metals the physical response is transformed and contracted directly, so
truncated degenerate empty-band projectors need not match. Representatives are
distributed among MPI ranks. Ground-state
and offset-state setup still use the full mesh. This reduction currently uses
translation-free operations that preserve q exactly, fix the displaced atom
without a cell shift, and map its direction to itself or its negative. Other
operations retain the full response mesh. Disabling q symmetries also disables
this reduction. Electron-phonon export can use this response reduction while
contracting matrices with the full saved electronic eigenstates.

`phonon-dfpt-solver nested` selects fixed-potential Sternheimer solves with outer
response-potential mixing, following the main structure of QE's DFPT solver.
The inner accuracy tightens as the coupled residual decreases, and each outer
iteration solves a correction about the previous response. Independent band
CG removes converged bands from subsequent Hamiltonian calls and uses a kinetic
preconditioner; nonpositive curvature or an inner iteration limit triggers a
MINRES solve. A regularized DIIS history mixes the induced potentials. Both
solver choices verify the same coupled response residual and Gamma charge
constraint. Set `phonon-dfpt-mixing 0.4 8 80` to specify the mixing fraction,
history length, and outer iteration limit (shown here with their defaults).
The default remains `phonon-dfpt-solver coupled`: nested iteration is not faster
for every material or mesh, so compare timings for the intended calculation.

To limit response memory, set `phonon-dfpt-k-batch-size 1` (or another positive
number of local k points per MPI rank). This selects fixed-potential k streaming:
every outer iteration uses the same potential for all batches, sums their induced
densities, then mixes the updated screening potential. Additional sweeps verify a
bound on the original coupled residual and contract the final forces. At metallic
Gamma, the fixed-electron-number projection is accumulated over the entire mesh.
All bands and k integration points are retained. This trades repeated offset-state
setup and response solves for less memory; it requires uniform full-mesh weights
and does not use the per-displacement k symmetry reduction. Ground-state
wavefunctions remain on the full mesh. Zero restores the ordinary solver.
This control can be combined with q checkpoint batches and changed when resuming
the same checkpoints. Q batches alone limit the work per job, while k streaming
limits the workspace within a response calculation.

GPU nonlocal projectors may also retain significant memory across k points.
`JDFTX_PROJECTOR_CACHE_MIB=256` bounds their retained cache per species and MPI
rank; the default remains unlimited. This cache limit changes recomputation,
not the pseudopotential or the response equations.

The ground-state SCF can use a symmetry-reduced k mesh. Save its `State` and
`Fillings`, then replace `initial-state` in the DFPT input with:
\code
phonon-dfpt-initial-state 01-scf.in scf.$VAR
\endcode
Here `01-scf.in` is the original symmetry-enabled SCF input. Include paths and
checkpoint paths resolve relative to the working directory, as in normal JDFTx
inputs. The target DFPT input retains `symmetries none`. Geometry, species order,
pseudopotential paths, cutoff, band count, smearing and weighted k mesh must match.
Wavefunctions are transformed onto the full mesh with reciprocal-index remapping,
space-group translation phases and time reversal, and distributed across MPI ranks.
The full ground state is polished before response calculations. Use ordinary
`initial-state` to read an existing full-mesh checkpoint.

To avoid the full ground-state expansion entirely, combine the reduced restart
with `phonon-dfpt-ibz-storage yes`, `phonon-dfpt-k-batch-size 1`, and
`dump End None`. The ground state is prepared on its original IBZ and its
representative wavefunctions then stay on the host. Each active GPU batch
reconstructs source and on-mesh offset states directly from those representatives,
including reciprocal-index remapping, translation phases, and time reversal.
No ground-state wavefunction array on the full mesh is allocated. All electronic
points and bands still contribute to each screening iteration. Explicit force
derivatives and final native electron-phonon export also run in k batches; the
matching full-mesh electronic checkpoint is written one state at a time.
Q groups synchronize the representative band gauge before export. Generic dumps
which require a complete electronic state are disabled in this mode; phonon
outputs and their matching electronic files are written by the driver.

IBZ storage also accepts a saved fixed-density NSCF input as the first argument
of `phonon-dfpt-initial-state`. That input must use `fix-electron-density` with
the original converged density, and the state pattern must provide wavefunctions,
fillings, and eigenvalues. The original density and potential stay fixed while
source-band residuals are checked and refined. No replacement SCF mesh is created.
The target geometry must match the actual symmetry-adjusted checkpoint geometry;
the loader checks it after source symmetry setup and rejects a mismatch. Source
and target FFT grids, functionals, pseudopotentials, smearing, band count and
weighted electronic meshes must agree. Native electron-phonon export still
requires a phonon q grid contained in the original electronic mesh.
The full electronic mesh must preserve every spatial symmetry used by the source
IBZ calculation. An anisotropic electronic mesh breaking those crystal symmetries
is rejected; use a compatible mesh or explicitly restrict the source symmetry group.

MPI ranks normally divide the electronic k points, with q and displacement
directions solved sequentially. Set `phonon-dfpt-q-groups 2` to split ranks into
two independent q groups. Each group solves different representative q points,
while its ranks divide the complete electronic k mesh. For example, four MPI
ranks and two q groups give two ranks per group. Group sizes may be unequal.
Each group initializes and polishes its own ground state from the same input;
this increases replicated memory and initialization work. Use no more groups
than MPI ranks, and preferably no more than the number of representative q
points. The q groups collect all force matrices before the common symmetry
reconstruction and Fourier transformation. Only the first group writes dumps,
and the final phonon matrices are written once. A failure in a group aborts the
parent calculation. The default is one q group. Atomic displacement directions
are still sequential within each group; GPU and CPU threads accelerate local
plane-wave operations. By default, representatives are assigned to groups by
estimated work (response count and off-mesh offset-state work), with larger
groups receiving more work. All displacements at a q stay together to preserve
offset-state and perturbation caches. The estimates are heuristics, not measured
execution times. `phonon-dfpt-balance-q-groups no` restores round-robin assignment.

Frozen-ground-state XC derivatives are reused across displacements at each q.
Bare displacement potentials are constructed once per sign and displacement,
rather than once for every electronic k point. Displacement source vectors are
also cached for reuse in force-matrix contractions. Their additional retained
storage is limited to 128 MiB per MPI rank by default; set
`phonon-dfpt-source-cache-size <MiB>` to change this limit. Zero disables source
storage while retaining the potential and XC caches. Cache admission uses the
largest rank's allocation so all ranks take the same collective paths, including
the metallic Gamma charge projection. All caches are released after the q point.
`phonon-dfpt-cache no` disables these caches for numerical validation. The log
reports source builds, hits and retained storage. Caching changes neither the
response equations nor their convergence thresholds.

For commensurate k +/- q points already on the electronic mesh, stored states
are copied from the converged ground state, including reciprocal wrapping. The
Hamiltonian residual is checked and states are refined only when necessary.
`phonon-dfpt-reuse-offset-states no` disables this optimization for validation.
Off-mesh offset states are minimized at fixed potential. If the nonlinear
minimizer leaves a large residual, a restarted block Davidson fallback expands
the trial subspace with independent vectors. Both paths must pass the offset
residual check; increasing `electronic-minimize nIterations` increases the
fallback iteration limit as well.

\subsection DFPTQBatches Completed-q checkpoints and separate GPU jobs

Add `phonon-dfpt-checkpoint responses` to save each completed symmetry-independent
q response in a directory. An ordinary calculation with this command reuses
completed responses and solves only those still missing. Checkpoints are published
by atomic rename after all displacement responses at a representative pass their
convergence and Hermiticity checks. An interrupted q must be recomputed; completed
q points survive. The parent directory must already exist.

For separate jobs, keep a common DFPT input (including the checkpoint directory)
and add one batch command to each job:
\code
# Job 1:
phonon-dfpt-q-batch 1 4
# Job 2 uses 2 4, job 3 uses 3 4, job 4 uses 4 4.
\endcode
The command takes a 1-based batch index and batch count. Representative ordinal r,
starting at zero in the logged representative list, belongs to batch `1 + r % count`.
Every symmetry orbit stays in one batch. Batches can run sequentially or concurrently
with disjoint GPU allocations, sharing the checkpoint directory. Use separate logs
and `dump End None` for these jobs. Do not run two writers for the same batch.
MPI q groups remain available within each batch, with electronic k points distributed
across ranks in each group. Idle batches are allowed when there are more batches
than representatives. Batch jobs write checkpoints only, without native phonon or
electron-phonon outputs. Rerunning a batch reuses its completed q responses.

After all batch jobs finish, remove the batch command and add:
\code
phonon-dfpt-merge yes
\endcode
This run checks for all required representatives and reconstructs symmetry partners,
reciprocal reality, the acoustic sum rule and the real-space interpolation files.
It does not solve perturbation responses. With `phonon-dfpt-electron-phonon yes`,
checkpoints contain the screened induced potentials for the independent displacement
directions. Merge contracts these potentials against a single common set of
ground-state eigenvectors and writes the native `phononHsub` and matching electronic
states. This avoids mixing incompatible band gauges from independent jobs. Merge
still initializes/polishes the ground state and performs the electron-phonon export;
it is not a lightweight file concatenation. Run only one merge for a given output
prefix after the batches finish.

CPU and CUDA executables support the same checkpoint format on the same architecture.
All response solving and final band contractions retain their existing GPU paths.
Completed displacement potentials are kept in host memory while subsequent responses
run, reducing retained GPU memory. Response wavefunction arrays are allocated only
when a missing q needs solving; initialization and merge avoid the unused
lattice-periodic response workspace. Completed checkpointed responses release
their wavefunction workspaces before the next q or export. Checkpoints add disk storage of approximately
`16 * Nfft * Nindependent_displacements` bytes per representative with electron-phonon
export, plus the force matrix, density reference and metadata. The final native
electron-phonon output has the same size as an unsplit calculation.

Use the same converged SCF state and identical physical/numerical inputs for all
jobs. Checkpoints validate parsed inputs (including response tolerances), realized
geometry, k/FFT meshes, pseudopotential contents, symmetry plans and the ground-state
density (relative L2 difference at most 1e-6). Input signatures conservatively retain
command order. Execution/output controls, checkpoint path and initial-state paths
may change; MPI rank/group counts and CPU/GPU choice may also change. Checksums
reject truncated or corrupt files. Use a fresh directory when changing the system
or numerical settings; partial `.tmp-*` files from interrupted writes are ignored.
Phonon cache budgets, cache/optimization switches, offset-state reuse and export
reciprocity may also change without invalidating completed responses.

Q batching limits work per job and enables restart. Each q still needs the entire
electronic integration mesh for self-consistent screening, so it does not remove
the memory requirement of one q response. Distributing k states across more GPU
ranks in one q group and lowering response/export cache budgets remain useful
when that single-response memory is the limiting factor.

The force matrix contains the self-consistent electronic response, explicit
second local and nonlocal pseudopotential derivatives, and an analytic finite-q
ionic Ewald Hessian. The finite-q GGA kernel uses the Bloch derivative
`gradient + i*q_cart`, including its divergence terms. Response residuals and
Hermiticity are checked before producing output. Reciprocal reality is enforced
between q and -q, and the Gamma matrix is projected onto the complement of the
three uniform Cartesian translations to impose the acoustic sum rule. The size
of this correction is reported; a large correction warrants checking convergence.

Output files:
- `Si.phononForceMatrixQ`: text, with the number of q points and modes, followed
  by each q and its complex row-major Cartesian force matrix in Hartree/bohr^2.
- `Si.phononCellMap`: integer lattice vectors and Cartesian cell offsets.
- `Si.phononCellWeights`: binary atom-pair Wigner-Seitz weights.
- `Si.phononOmegaSq`: little-endian binary doubles, a column-major mass-weighted
  matrix for each cell in cell-map order, with Wigner-Seitz weights included.
  Eigenvalues are squared frequencies in atomic units.
- `Si.phononBasis`: atom identities, Cartesian mass-weighted displacement
  directions and masses in amu.

The cell-phase convention is `u_s(R) = u_s(q) exp(2*pi*i*q.R)`, without an extra
basis-position phase. The discrete inverse transform is
`Phi(R) = sum_q exp(-2*pi*i*q.R) C(q)/Nq`. Equivalent boundary cells are weighted
using the existing Wigner-Seitz cell map. After mass weighting,
`D(q) = sum_R exp(2*pi*i*q.R) OmegaSq(R)` reproduces the coarse q matrices.
Interpolate the matrices first, then diagonalize; do not interpolate eigenvalues
or eigenvectors independently.

Create a text file `qpath.dat` with three fractional q coordinates per row, then
run the NumPy-based utility:
\code
python3 /path/to/jdftx/scripts/phononDFPT Si. qpath.dat -o Si.frequencies.dat
\endcode
The output contains q followed by all mode frequencies in inverse centimeters.
A negative number denotes an imaginary frequency. `--matrices Si.phonons.npz`
also saves complex dynamical matrices, squared frequencies and eigenvectors.
The utility reads the same lattice-space files as the supercell phonon workflow.

\section DFPTElectronPhonon Electron-phonon matrix elements

Add `phonon-dfpt-electron-phonon yes` to export the fully screened
Hamiltonian derivatives in the same `phononHsub` format as the native
supercell phonon executable. The operator includes the ionic local and nonlocal
pseudopotential derivatives and the induced Hartree and XC potential. This
output works with periodic or slab Coulomb, including slab truncation embedding.
The metallic Gamma response includes fixed-number occupation/Fermi-level
screening; the Hamiltonian derivative is not shifted by minus d(mu).

The electronic mesh must be Gamma centered and contain the phonon q grid:
each `kpoint-folding` dimension must be divisible by the corresponding
`phonon-dfpt` dimension. The output uses the Nq electronic k points commensurate
with that phonon supercell, even when the electronic integration mesh is denser.
Incompatible meshes are rejected before response calculations. Phonon-only DFPT
continues to support q points off the electronic mesh.

The output prefix has these additional files:
- `phononHsub`: binary complex128 matrices, with outer loops over Cartesian
  mode, target k, source k, then a column-major band matrix. Rows are final
  bands and columns are initial bands. The target/source k difference selects
  the phonon q. Each matrix is `dH_a(k_target,k_source)/(Nq*sqrt(M_a))`,
  where the Cartesian derivative uses unit displacements in bohrs and M is
  in electron masses. The 1/Nq factor is the supercell normalization of a
  displacement localized in one cell. No phonon frequency or zero-point
  normalization is included; this matches the native phonon output exactly.
- `phononKpts`: the ordered commensurate electronic k subset, in centered
  reciprocal coordinates, with the same ordering as the native phonon code.
- `wfns`, `eigenvals`, `fillings`: the matching ground-state eigenvectors,
  energies and occupations for the complete electronic mesh, written even
  with `dump End None`. Use this DFPT prefix for Wannier input so the band
  rotations and matrix elements refer to exactly the same electronic gauge.

The existing `phononBasis` supplies species, atom index, mass-weighted Cartesian
displacement and atomic mass. All stored electronic bands are exported.
A fully occupied insulating input supplies only valence-band elements. To
include conduction bands, use Fermi/Gauss smearing and sufficient `elec-n-bands`
in both SCF and DFPT. The example in `examples/dfpt-silicon-electron-phonon`
includes empty bands and a Wannier input.

Export retains q-point and Cartesian displacement symmetry reductions. Only
independent responses are solved; their induced local potentials are transformed
on the FFT grid with atom-cell phases, fractional translations, reciprocal q
wrapping and time reversal. The symmetry images span the displacement space;
the full set of induced potentials is recovered by a Gram-matrix inverse.
Bare local and nonlocal ionic derivatives are evaluated analytically for every
target displacement. Matrix elements are contracted directly in the saved
full-mesh electronic eigenstates, preserving their phases and arbitrary
degenerate-band rotations without electronic sewing-matrix reconstruction.
Response k-point reduction is independently available with
`phonon-dfpt-k-symmetries yes`. Disable
`phonon-dfpt-symmetries` and `phonon-dfpt-displacement-symmetries` for an explicit
comparison. Offset-state reuse and symmetry-reduced SCF unfolding remain
available. MPI q groups own complete q orbits and synchronize their source
Hamiltonian eigenvectors, including phases and degenerate-subspace rotations,
and write disjoint target/source blocks of a single `phononHsub` file.
Storage for this file is `16*(3*Natoms)*Nq^2*Nbands^2` bytes. The former
`phononEphQ...` and metadata files are no longer produced.

`phonon-dfpt-optimize yes` (default) applies complex local response potentials
directly, fuses bare and induced local export terms, and contracts nonlocal
projector derivatives in band space. `no` retains the reference operator path.
After force contractions, response vectors and source caches are released.
The exporter then caches real-space source wavefunctions with a per-rank limit
of `phonon-dfpt-eph-cache-size 512` MiB; zero disables this cache. Source
projector overlaps are retained separately for the current q orbit.
`phonon-dfpt-eph-reciprocity yes` (default) evaluates one unordered k pair and
writes its Hermitian partner when q and -q belong to the same owned orbit.
Otherwise, full contractions are used. These settings preserve the file layout,
normalization, double precision, and complete nonlocal derivatives.

For scalar GPU local-potential applications, reusable batched cuFFT plans and
shared scratch storage replace per-band transforms. Set the environment
variable `JDFTX_FFT_BATCH_SIZE` to a maximum batch size (default 32, maximum 256).
Zero selects the original per-band operator; cached export still uses a
single-band scratch transform. FFT grids, scatter/gather bases and
normalization are unchanged. Potential reconstruction and displacement mixing
stay on the GPU, and matrices are reduced to the group head in one batch per
displacement. Allocation-pool reports at phase boundaries distinguish live
blocks from cached blocks and count underlying allocations. Pool bytes are
allocation capacities, not total GPU-driver memory usage. Q-group balancing
also includes a modest estimate for the export orbit size.

In a Wannier input with the same cell, ions, pseudopotentials, cutoff, bands,
electronic mesh and `symmetries none`, set, for example:
\code
wannier phononSupercell 6 6 1
wannier-initial-state phonon.$VAR
wannier-dump-name wannier.$VAR
# Add the wannier-center and band-window settings for your material.
\endcode
The supercell dimensions must equal those in `phonon-dfpt`. Run the existing
`wannier` or `wannier_gpu` executable to obtain `mlwfHePh`, phonon cell maps
and cell weights for interpolation. No file-format converter is needed.
Wannier applies its usual translational-invariance correction and electronic
rotation before the real-space transform. Construct phonon eigenvectors and
apply the zero-point factor when evaluating mode-resolved couplings.

Validation (`dfptElectronPhonon`, `dfptElectronPhononMPI` and
`dfptElectronPhononEmbeddedSlab`) checks native file dimensions and k ordering,
mass/supercell normalization, screened band derivatives against independent
SCF finite differences, complex target/source reciprocity, and an actual
Wannier export. The periodic test also compares all complex matrices with an
independent native frozen-phonon calculation. MPI testing verifies complex
reciprocity across q groups and compares the resulting Wannier couplings.

`dfptElectronPhononSymmetry` and its MPI variant compare complex exported
operators with explicit responses on a nonsymmorphic silicon q grid.
The Nb variants compare all complete stored band manifolds after aligning
the exported eigenstate gauges; the rotations test covers Cartesian mixing
in hexagonal BN. All tests also check complex reciprocity and force matrices.

Polar crystals require special care: the Gamma calculation uses zero macroscopic
electric field. This implementation does not compute Born effective charges or
subtract and restore the long-range dipole contribution for interpolation.
Consequently the Fourier interpolation does not capture the directional
nonanalytic LO-TO limit at Gamma in polar materials. See Sec. II.D.2-3 of the
review before applying ordinary finite-mesh interpolation to those systems.

Validation is available through CTest (`dfptEwaldSolver`, `dfptInterpolation`,
`dfptRestart`, and `dfptResponse` / `dfptResponseLDA` when SG15 H is found).
`dfptQSymmetry`, enabled when SG15 Si is found, compares reconstructed complex
matrices with explicit solves on an anisotropic q grid and checks the Fourier
round trip. `dfptQParallel` additionally compares MPI q-group configurations,
including unequal and idle groups, end-state dumps and group failure handling.
`dfptMainSpeedups` and `dfptMainSpeedupsNb` compare full-mesh coupled, reduced-k,
nested, and combined response calculations. `dfptMainSpeedupsNbMPI` also checks
unequal MPI groups and Gamma charge conservation; `dfptKSymmetryNbDense` covers
truncated degenerate empty bands on a 64-point Nb mesh. `dfptFrozenCache` and its
Nb/MPI variants check cache budgets and cached/uncached force matrices. These
tests record timings without requiring a speedup on every machine.
`DFPT_H_PSEUDO` is a CMake cache path to
`H_ONCV_PBE.upf` for the physical response regression. To additionally compare an
uneven MPI distribution against serial results:
\code
DFPT_MPI_LAUNCH="mpirun -n 2" python3 jdftx/test/dfpt/checkResponse.py build /path/to/H_ONCV_PBE.upf
\endcode
The physical regression compares real and imaginary parts of a nonzero-q GGA
force matrix with independently minimized, sinusoidally displaced supercell
forces. It also checks acoustic translations and the Fourier grid round trip.
*/
